\title{DFS introduction and intuition}
\date{2026-09-17T15:35:44Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{The first graph algorithm we will learn about can answer simple questions of connectivity: given a graph #{G} and particular vertices #{s} and #{t}, are they connected by a path? If so, what is one such path?}

\p{The basic idea is to explore as far as you can in a certain direction before backtracking and continuing to explore further unexplored paths, and so on, just like you might explore a physical maze. The problem is loops: we need some way to mark vertices that we have already visited, so we know if we have been somewhere before and don’t get stuck exploring a loop forever.  Just like we might mark where we have been by placing rocks on the ground in a physical maze, we can mark previously visited vertices with "digital rocks" so we don't get caught in a loop.}
